  "summary_text": "Kyle, Port Washington is running hot and pulling ahead; Hicksville is underperforming structurally and needs immediate attention on capacity and retail execution.\n\nPort Washington had a strong Monday\u2014$4,165 total revenue, up 36% YoY on transaction count, with 6 groomers producing $557 per groomer against a trailing-30-day baseline of $474. That's 18% above pace and the real signal here: your team is executing. Month-to-date you're tracking +6.4% against DOW-adjusted expectations, which means the uptick is genuine performance, not calendar luck. Core grooming revenue jumped 41% week-over-week. One concern: retail dropped 9% despite higher traffic, suggesting front-of-house isn't capturing the add-on opportunity as consistently as it should\u2014attach rate held at 57% but retail mix weakened. Your lapse pool at Port is substantial ($296k annual value across 469 customers), with 118 actively drifting; this is reactivation upside if you can deploy targeted outreach. Inventory gaps on bestsellers like X-Small Treat Tables and Bully Sticks are creating friction.\n\nHicksville is a different story. Yesterday's $629 revenue was down 21% week-over-week on just one groomer, and while you're up 13% MTD DOW-adjusted, that masks a structural problem: you're running at 2.4 groomer-days per day versus Port's 4.9. Retail cratered 51% YoY\u2014this isn't traffic; this is either missed upsell or a front-of-house focus gap. Add-on attach collapsed from 75% to 40%. Hicksville's $/groomer-day ($429 MTD) lags Port ($464 MTD) and your trailing-30-day baseline ($434), meaning even per-unit productivity is softer. You have a smaller lapse pool here ($91k), but 53 customers are actively drifting\u2014retention risk.\n\n**Capacity & coverage: Port Washington has 7 groomers scheduled today and 7 tomorrow with 15 bather hours; Hicksville has 3 groomers today and 3 tomorrow with zero bather hours. Next 7 days: Port 37 groomer-days, Hicksville 18 groomer-days\u2014your capacity gap is widening, and Hicksville's lack of bather support is constraining throughput.**"
